> program to do some accurate time counting. Ideally, I would like to
> access the rpb_cc_freq field Hardware Restart Parameter Block (rpb) as
> possible under OSF/1 using getsysinfo(2) or the calibrated
> cycles_per_sec variable from sys/alpha/alpha/machdep.c.
> 
> While it would be easy to introduce a new sysctl or similar, I was
> wondering if there is already a 'standard' way under FreeBSD to access
> this information (ideally, also under ix86).
> 
> I searched around a bit, but couldn't find any obvious way.

The closest you will find is hw.cpu_mhz, via sysctl.

I am personally not very happy at all with what has happened to
the clock code; what used to be a direct reference to "curtime"
is now a convoluted mess requiring a dead chicken and some pagan
incantations to achieve the same thing, at the same time making
gettimeofday() inordinately expensive.

It also bothers me that the standard system clock doesn't use the
cycle counter, and instead is rn of the i8254 interrupt, which
bothers me no end.

If you could get a baseline and a frequency in user space, it would
be very easy to implement gettimeofday() in user space, if things
were not so strange.


